WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.228 --> 00:00:02.770
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So you want
to harness the power of machine

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.443
learning for your business.

00:00:04.443 --> 00:00:06.610
Well, one of the first
problems you'll need to solve

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.520
is what data you'll be
using to teach your models.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.860
So you guys have
like a mantra, right?

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.830
SPEAKER: Yes no data, no ML.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI: What
does that mean exactly?

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:18.730
SPEAKER: Well,
your model is only

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:20.530
as good as the
data it trains on.

00:00:26.403 --> 00:00:28.570
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Luckily,
our professional services

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.310
ML team has refined a
set of best practices

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.130
for selecting and
preparing top quality data.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.987
And we're itching to
hand the goods over.

00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:37.820
SPEAKER: Is this the
part where we tell them

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:38.790
all of our secrets?

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That comes
up in a little bit later.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.832
First, we'll reveal
what goes into choosing

00:00:42.832 --> 00:00:45.470
a highly effective
data source, and later,

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.840
learn how to spot the
most common oversights

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.440
our professional services ML
team helps correct for clients.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.660
So ready to become an
independent authority

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:54.710
on good data?

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:56.210
Let's dive in.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.910
Rule number 1, clear labels.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.430
Many companies use
machine learning

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.350
because they want to
predict some outcome,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.800
from detecting credit card fraud
to forecasting ice cream sales,

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.993
from recommendation systems
to self-driving cars.

00:01:09.993 --> 00:01:11.660
Whatever it is you're
trying to predict,

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.550
you'll need to feed the
machine some examples

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.080
with known outcomes.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.963
We call those labels.

00:01:16.963 --> 00:01:18.380
If you were teaching
a machine how

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.550
to predict credit card
fraud, for instance,

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.040
you would tell the machine
for each new piece of data

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.960
whether or not fraud occurred.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.860
This is what we mean
by known outcomes.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.070
It may seem like a small point,
but for businesses looking

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.990
to take advantage of ML to
make accurate predictions,

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.290
these labeled examples
are what allow algorithms

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.808
to learn in the first place.

00:01:37.808 --> 00:01:39.350
SPEAKER: I would
say this is probably

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:42.860
the number one piece of
advice we give to our clients.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:44.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Labels?

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.610
SPEAKER: Labels, labels, labels.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.920
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Is
there anything else?

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.650
SPEAKER: Well, although more
data is generally better,

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.262
it has to be the
right kind of data.

00:01:53.262 --> 00:01:55.220
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That
sounds pretty important.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.530
Maybe like rule
number two important?

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.130
Rule number 2, although more
data is generally better,

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.818
it has to be the
right kind of data.

00:02:03.818 --> 00:02:06.110
Continuing with our example,
if you want your algorithm

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:09.289
to predict credit card fraud,
imagine all the information

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.440
a human being might rely
on to do the same task.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.720
Data like the amount
of the transaction

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.340
and where it took
place are definitely

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.680
useful, but cardholder name?

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.840
That may not be as helpful here.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.420
If you're not sure, erring
on the side of more data

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.980
is always wise.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.430
In the world of
machine learning,

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.700
we don't always know with
100% certainty what's going

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.470
to be relevant ahead of time.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:32.780
Expect to refine as you go.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.290
Sometimes relevant
also means granular.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.990
Suppose you wanted to predict
weather on an hourly basis,

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:43.040
but your data is only collected
daily, let's say 3:00 PM.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.190
Your model will learn how to
predict the weather, sure.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:47.870
But except for those
of us who decide

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.270
what to wear based on the
temperature outside at exactly

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:55.250
3:00 PM, the model won't be
as helpful as it could be.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.342
This also means collecting
data long enough.

00:02:57.342 --> 00:02:59.050
So if the target you're
trying to predict

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.300
is periodic, like
ice cream sales,

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.980
you'll need enough
cycles for your model

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.930
to discover the general trend.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.270
A good rule of thumb
is at least three.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.730
This table includes
plenty of helpful columns,

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.820
but which one that ML models
depend on to make accurate

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:14.974
predictions is still missing.

00:03:14.974 --> 00:03:18.292
[CLOCK TICKING]

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.830
You guessed labels, right?

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:21.820
Just checking.

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:25.420
Rule number 3, beware
of biased data.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.960
Granularity matters.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.810
But some errors
only become obvious

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.625
when we take a big
step backwards.

00:03:30.625 --> 00:03:32.500
It's just like how we
can tell when a picture

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.330
frame is leaning
to one side when

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.280
we're standing far enough away.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.560
Here, we'll define
bias as anytime

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.740
you're training data
skews towards some groups

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:43.570
more than the rest.

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.630
Let's say you're trying to
predict customer satisfaction.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.820
Well, we know that
online reviews

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:52.010
tend to overrepresent
the most extreme views.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.060
So if you choose to train a
model on these reviews alone,

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.930
your model could become biased.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.020
Now, don't be alarmed if
you have missing values

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.490
in a large data set.

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.770
But you should check to
see whether these missing

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:06.040
values frequently leave
out the same group.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.260
You might think that
simply collecting more data

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.910
will solve the problem, but
that's not always possible.

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:14.380
And even if it were, that's
only a part of the solution.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.300
Just as important,
if you have a way

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.820
to tell what dynamics
are causing the bias,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.829
you may still be able
to quantify the effect

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:23.680
and correct it by,
for example, adjusting

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.700
the weighing and different
samples accordingly.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.450
Expect to do some
corrections of your data

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.020
set, and don't let perfect
be the enemy of great.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.300
Congratulations, you're
more than halfway

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.980
to maximizing the power
of your training data.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.870
Now let's finish strong
with some data preparation.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.553
Rule number 4, use
consistent terms.

00:04:44.553 --> 00:04:46.720
You'll want to make sure
you're using the same units

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.100
across your data set.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.920
Check not only for
quantitative units like price,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.780
but for categorical
units as well.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.530
This also means making sure the
meaning of the data in a given

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.720
column is consistent.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.510
If a single price column
switches between wholesale

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:04.300
and retail price, and
you're missing a column that

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.330
identifies which is which, your
model will treat these numbers

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.600
as though they measure the same
thing, even though they don't.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:14.530
Rule number 5,
out-think data leakage.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.302
Data leakage happens when you
train your model on information

00:05:17.302 --> 00:05:19.510
that won't be available when
it's time for your model

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:21.500
to actually do its job.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.570
So here's my favorite example.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:25.000
Let's say you're
teaching a model

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.670
to pick out which acoustic
recordings contain calls

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.470
from an endangered
species of whale.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.290
[WHALE CALL]

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.470
What if the recordings
with the right audio

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.170
happened to be the same
file size, but only

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.700
due to some processing
that was done on them?

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.020
Your model could learn to rely
on file size instead of sound,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.270
and later, when it's time
to start making predictions,

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.660
label all kinds of
sounds as whale.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:49.600
Almost done.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:52.350
Let's quickly review
the basic principles.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.410
Labels, labels, labels;
more data is good,

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.490
but it has to be the right
kind; beware of biased data;

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.250
use consistent terms
throughout your spreadsheets;

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.620
and rule out data leakage.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.950
And voila, you're ready
to select the right data

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.450
and make sure it's cooked and
good for your machine learning

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:10.590
algorithm.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.540
For a more detailed
explanation, check out

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.210
the full article on the
Google Cloud Platform blog

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:19.080
and find the link to our ML data
prep check list at the bottom.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.950
Have a tip you'd like
to share with us?

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:22.450
Join the conversation
on our Twitter

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.990
handle using #mldataprep.

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.790
And as always,
thanks for watching.

